{"status":"abandon","summary":"I'm abandoning this: the fast path builds and works, but it has nothing to speed up in q21 on the current best. q21 bench: 960.4ms → 984.8ms (+2.5%, within noise).

What I changed: in src/planner/filter/bloom_filter.cpp, BFTableFilter::Filter now has a fast path for flat, NULL-free integer keys (INT8..INT64, UINT8..UINT64). It computes duckdb::Hash<T> inline, the same function VectorOperations::Hash uses, and reuses the previous lookup result while the key equals the previous key. Any other input uses the existing path. There are no header changes.

Why it has no effect: the issue assumed the l2/l3 lineitem scans carry `IN BF(l_orderkey)`. A JSON profile of the current best shows they only have min/max dynamic filters (`l_orderkey>=5 AND l_orderkey<=59999777`), and they cost 0.21s and 0.35s, not 0.72s and 0.86s. The only BF probe in q21 is on the orders scan (o_orderkey), whose keys are unique, so there are no runs of equal keys to skip. The profile the hypothesis relied on came from an experiment that pushed BFs into the subquery scans and was never landed.","generality":"This would help join-filter BF probes on integer columns that are clustered or sorted on the probe key, but only where a BF is actually pushed onto such a scan. It does nothing for unique-key scans (orders here), for non-integer keys, or for inputs that contain NULLs or are not flat vectors (those use the old path).","notes":"The premise was stale: the current best has no BF on the q21 l2/l3 scans. Pushing BFs into those scans was tried earlier and gave 0%. That BF made the scans more expensive (l2 0.96s, l3 1.23s), and this fast path would partly undo that cost. So the two together might show a small gain, but neither shows one alone. Before planning BF-probe work, check the current best's JSON profile for `IN BF(`. The rest of q21's time is in the HASH_JOINs: the l1 build/probe at 1.2s and the subquery joins at 1.0-1.2s. Those are the larger targets."}
